1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,292
Two weeks ago, this project built something that runs two AI agents

2
00:00:03,292 --> 00:00:06,000
on the same company and flags when their numbers disagree.

3
00:00:06,708 --> 00:00:09,125
Last week, both agents finally became real.

4
00:00:09,542 --> 00:00:12,333
This week, it ran for the first time. And the first thing

5
00:00:12,333 --> 00:00:15,167
it produced wasn't a disagreement. It was a number that came from

6
00:00:15,167 --> 00:00:15,967
nowhere.

7
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:17,100
Fast version.

8
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:18,842
Cross-Agent Validation runs two

9
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:22,133
independently-reasoning agents on one company, pulls the numbers out of each

10
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:24,383
conclusion, and flags anything that doesn't match.

11
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:28,467
Set arithmetic, no model, no judge. It started with one real agent

12
00:00:28,467 --> 00:00:32,133
and one fixture, a hand-written stand-in you configure with whatever answer you

13
00:00:32,133 --> 00:00:35,300
want, used on purpose to prove the detector works before pointing it

14
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:36,100
at anything real.

15
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:37,350
Last week,

16
00:00:37,633 --> 00:00:40,633
the fixture was replaced with a second real grader reading the same

17
00:00:40,633 --> 00:00:45,342
SEC filing through a different lens: balance sheet versus earnings quality. A

18
00:00:45,342 --> 00:00:49,467
hundred forty-three tests passed. Zero lines changed in the comparator itself.

19
00:00:49,842 --> 00:00:53,008
But nobody had actually watched two real agents talk to each other

20
00:00:53,008 --> 00:00:53,808
yet.

21
00:00:53,930 --> 00:00:56,180
The second agent ran on a model set up locally.

22
00:00:56,638 --> 00:00:58,513
Once it ran: real filing data went in.

23
00:00:59,013 --> 00:01:01,847
A real independent model came back with a conclusion for each side.

24
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:05,430
Producer A, reading assets, revenue, and net income, wrote: 'Calculated the

25
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:09,097
debt-to-equity ratio as zero point three four.' Its input data contained no

26
00:01:09,097 --> 00:01:12,722
debt and no equity figures. Not close, not derived, the concept wasn't

27
00:01:12,722 --> 00:01:13,522
in the data at all.

28
00:01:13,888 --> 00:01:15,763
Producer B, reading real earnings figures,

29
00:01:16,472 --> 00:01:20,555
cited zero numbers. It described them, 'consistent,' 'significantly large,' but

30
00:01:20,555 --> 00:01:21,638
never wrote one down.

31
00:01:22,013 --> 00:01:23,763
The system flagged a contradiction.

32
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:26,430
It was right to. But not for the reason you'd hope.

33
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:28,747
One surprising result could be noise.

34
00:01:29,247 --> 00:01:32,330
So five separate tests ran against it, not a quick pass or

35
00:01:32,330 --> 00:01:33,830
fail on each, but the full treatment:

36
00:01:34,247 --> 00:01:36,580
what the test is, why it was run, what a clean result

37
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:39,663
should look like, exactly what was fed into it, what actually came

38
00:01:39,663 --> 00:01:40,747
back, and what that means.

39
00:01:41,288 --> 00:01:42,122
Here's all five.

40
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:44,225
Test one: claim verification.

41
00:01:44,683 --> 00:01:47,517
Here's what it is: for every citation in a thought_log that points

42
00:01:47,517 --> 00:01:48,392
at a real URL,

43
00:01:48,850 --> 00:01:52,058
this mechanism fetches that source and checks whether the numbers being claimed

44
00:01:52,058 --> 00:01:54,475
actually appear in it, within one percent tolerance.

45
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:55,975
Here's why it exists:

46
00:01:56,350 --> 00:01:59,433
this is the system's dedicated defense against exactly this failure,

47
00:01:59,808 --> 00:02:02,600
a citation that looks real but isn't backed by what it points

48
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:03,400
to.

49
00:02:03,517 --> 00:02:06,267
Here's what a clean result looks like: a true number should confirm

50
00:02:06,267 --> 00:02:07,067
against the source.

51
00:02:07,558 --> 00:02:08,975
A fabricated number should come back

52
00:02:09,517 --> 00:02:13,183
checked-and-not-found. Either outcome means the mechanism is doing its job, a

53
00:02:13,183 --> 00:02:16,183
hundred percent pass rate isn't the goal, a hundred percent honest verdict

54
00:02:16,183 --> 00:02:19,558
is. Here's what it was actually given: the citation under test was

55
00:02:19,558 --> 00:02:23,392
the debt-to-equity line, pointing at the same SEC filing already fetched for

56
00:02:23,392 --> 00:02:24,808
Producer A's real inputs.

57
00:02:25,433 --> 00:02:28,683
Here's what happened: verification never got the chance to check that number

58
00:02:28,683 --> 00:02:29,483
at all.

59
00:02:29,475 --> 00:02:33,433
One layer upstream, claim extraction only recognizes figures shaped like a dollar

60
00:02:33,433 --> 00:02:36,058
amount, a percentage, a multiple, or basis points.

61
00:02:36,683 --> 00:02:40,475
A bare decimal ratio, zero point three four, no symbol attached, matched

62
00:02:40,475 --> 00:02:41,308
none of those patterns.

63
00:02:41,683 --> 00:02:43,892
It was invisible before verification ever ran.

64
00:02:44,267 --> 00:02:45,067
Here's what it means:

65
00:02:45,225 --> 00:02:48,558
a verification layer is only as reliable as what gets handed to

66
00:02:48,558 --> 00:02:49,358
it.

67
00:02:49,183 --> 00:02:52,767
A gap in extraction can silently starve a working checker of the

68
00:02:52,767 --> 00:02:54,308
one claim it most needed to see.

69
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:58,623
Test two: determinism. What it is: run the identical question through the

70
00:02:58,623 --> 00:03:02,832
identical model, same temperature, same fixed seed, five separate times, and compare

71
00:03:02,832 --> 00:03:03,748
all five answers.

72
00:03:04,290 --> 00:03:07,707
Why it matters: it separates two very different problems. A wrong answer

73
00:03:07,707 --> 00:03:10,790
that changes every time is noise, annoying, but survivable.

74
00:03:11,207 --> 00:03:14,332
A wrong answer that repeats identically is a stable pattern in the

75
00:03:14,332 --> 00:03:16,873
model's reasoning, worse, but at least diagnosable.

76
00:03:17,415 --> 00:03:20,498
What a clean result looks like: either all five converge on the

77
00:03:20,498 --> 00:03:24,415
same, hopefully correct, answer, or, if something's wrong, the same wrong answer

78
00:03:24,415 --> 00:03:27,832
keeps recurring, because a repeatable failure is one you can actually target.

79
00:03:28,457 --> 00:03:29,457
What was actually given:

80
00:03:29,873 --> 00:03:33,498
the project's own local-model adapter defaults to temperature zero and a fixed

81
00:03:33,498 --> 00:03:34,298
seed of

82
00:03:34,332 --> 00:03:37,582
forty-two, that part is confirmed straight from the adapter's source code.

83
00:03:38,123 --> 00:03:41,790
Whether this specific run used those defaults unmodified isn't independently

84
00:03:41,790 --> 00:03:43,082
confirmed from this checkout,

85
00:03:43,457 --> 00:03:45,165
flagged plainly, not glossed over.

86
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:48,707
What happened: four of the five runs converged on the same invented

87
00:03:48,707 --> 00:03:51,873
pair of ratios, off by one digit in the last decimal place.

88
00:03:52,373 --> 00:03:55,415
The original debt-to-equity number, the one from the first run, never came

89
00:03:55,415 --> 00:03:56,540
back across the other four.

90
00:03:56,957 --> 00:04:00,415
What it means: fixing temperature and seed narrows the model's behavior,

91
00:04:00,998 --> 00:04:04,707
but doesn't collapse it to one single deterministic answer, there's still an

92
00:04:04,707 --> 00:04:05,507
outlier.

93
00:04:05,623 --> 00:04:09,040
And the outlier was exactly the fabrication this video is about.

94
00:04:09,498 --> 00:04:12,415
Seen only once, it would have looked like the model's normal behavior

95
00:04:12,415 --> 00:04:14,915
instead of the one time it strayed furthest from it.

96
00:04:15,510 --> 00:04:19,010
Test three: the project's own consistency probe, a separate mechanism from

97
00:04:19,010 --> 00:04:21,510
determinism, built for exactly this situation.

98
00:04:22,135 --> 00:04:24,843
What it is: run a second, fully independent pass on the same

99
00:04:24,843 --> 00:04:28,718
input, then score how much the two conclusions actually overlap, words, weighted

100
00:04:28,718 --> 00:04:32,802
lightly at point four, specific numbers, weighted heavily at point six, because

101
00:04:32,802 --> 00:04:36,052
a real number is much harder to fabricate identically twice than a

102
00:04:36,052 --> 00:04:37,052
general phrase is.

103
00:04:37,510 --> 00:04:41,135
Why it exists: unlike verification, it needs no external source at all,

104
00:04:41,343 --> 00:04:45,010
genuine reasoning from real evidence should converge across independent runs on its

105
00:04:45,010 --> 00:04:45,810
own.

106
00:04:45,927 --> 00:04:48,468
What a clean result looks like: high agreement, and, this is the

107
00:04:48,468 --> 00:04:51,677
important part, a specific hard flag the moment any number shows up

108
00:04:51,677 --> 00:04:54,260
in one run and never in the other, no scoring required.

109
00:04:54,802 --> 00:04:58,135
What it was given: the same subject, the same agent identity, a

110
00:04:58,135 --> 00:04:59,177
fresh independent run,

111
00:04:59,593 --> 00:05:02,427
weights of point four on word overlap and point six on number

112
00:05:02,427 --> 00:05:03,227
overlap.

113
00:05:03,177 --> 00:05:06,552
What happened: the debt-to-equity figure appeared exactly once across every run

114
00:05:06,552 --> 00:05:08,135
collected, never a second time.

115
00:05:08,593 --> 00:05:12,302
The hard divergence flag fires exactly the way it's designed to. What

116
00:05:12,302 --> 00:05:14,927
it means: of all five tests here, this is the one that

117
00:05:14,927 --> 00:05:17,677
worked precisely as intended, with no workaround needed.

118
00:05:18,052 --> 00:05:20,093
It didn't need to know anything about the real world.

119
00:05:20,593 --> 00:05:24,302
It only needed the fabrication to fail at being consistent with itself,

120
00:05:24,677 --> 00:05:25,477
and it did.

121
00:05:25,930 --> 00:05:28,680
Test four: a guardrail stress test, and it's the odd one out,

122
00:05:28,722 --> 00:05:30,347
it isn't testing whether the agents are right.

123
00:05:30,805 --> 00:05:33,513
It's testing whether they can even speak the required format at all.

124
00:05:33,888 --> 00:05:36,347
Why that matters on its own: every other test here assumes the

125
00:05:36,347 --> 00:05:39,847
response actually parsed into a valid structure to begin with. If that

126
00:05:39,847 --> 00:05:41,763
layer breaks, nothing downstream even runs.

127
00:05:42,138 --> 00:05:45,472
What a clean result looks like: a first-attempt parse success rate at

128
00:05:45,472 --> 00:05:47,972
or near a hundred percent, and zero forced halts,

129
00:05:48,430 --> 00:05:51,305
proof the format-enforcement layer holds up against real, not

130
00:05:51,763 --> 00:05:55,555
fixture-generated, model output. What it was given: twenty-four real calls across

131
00:05:55,555 --> 00:05:58,805
the live batch, the same directive version each time, first attempt only

132
00:05:58,805 --> 00:06:00,930
counted, a retry wouldn't count as a clean pass.

133
00:06:01,430 --> 00:06:05,430
What happened: twenty-four for twenty-four. Zero retries triggered. Zero halts. What

134
00:06:05,430 --> 00:06:07,930
it means: whatever else is wrong this week, it isn't the format

135
00:06:07,930 --> 00:06:09,555
layer. That part held perfectly,

136
00:06:10,263 --> 00:06:11,888
at least at this sample size.

137
00:06:12,305 --> 00:06:15,847
The failure lives entirely in what the model reasoned, not in whether

138
00:06:15,847 --> 00:06:19,013
it could describe that reasoning in the shape the pipeline requires.

139
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:23,297
Test five: breadth. What it is: run the same two-producer comparison across

140
00:06:23,463 --> 00:06:26,422
twelve different real companies, not just the one from the first live

141
00:06:26,422 --> 00:06:27,222
run.

142
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:30,213
Why: one company could be a fluke, an unusual filing, a missing

143
00:06:30,213 --> 00:06:33,755
field, anything. Testing across a spread of real companies is what tells

144
00:06:33,755 --> 00:06:36,547
you whether a pattern is general or a one-off. What a clean

145
00:06:36,547 --> 00:06:37,463
result looks like:

146
00:06:37,838 --> 00:06:40,297
flags that correlate with actual factual contradictions,

147
00:06:40,963 --> 00:06:41,763
a low

148
00:06:41,630 --> 00:06:45,505
false-positive rate across a genuinely diverse batch of real filings. What it

149
00:06:45,505 --> 00:06:49,338
was given: twelve real tickers, real SEC filings pulled for each, both

150
00:06:49,338 --> 00:06:52,963
producers run per company, the existing contradiction flag left completely

151
00:06:52,963 --> 00:06:53,763
unmodified.

152
00:06:54,047 --> 00:06:57,463
What happened: eleven of twelve got flagged. The one exception was the

153
00:06:57,463 --> 00:07:00,880
single company where neither agent cited a number at all, nothing to

154
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:04,630
compare, so nothing to flag. And manually checking one of those eleven

155
00:07:04,672 --> 00:07:08,880
flagged cases showed both agents were completely correct, one reported assets and

156
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:13,130
revenue, the other reported earnings per share. Different concepts, zero actual

157
00:07:13,130 --> 00:07:15,005
conflict, and the flag fired anyway.

158
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:17,963
What it means, and this is the part that actually redirected the

159
00:07:17,963 --> 00:07:21,505
project this week: eleven out of twelve looked like an alarming contradiction

160
00:07:21,505 --> 00:07:22,713
rate at first glance.

161
00:07:23,422 --> 00:07:26,547
It was actually revealing that the flag can't yet tell the difference

162
00:07:26,547 --> 00:07:31,047
between two agents disagreeing and two agents simply talking about different things.

163
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:35,693
Five tests. One upstream extraction gap, one informative outlier,

164
00:07:36,068 --> 00:07:38,443
one mechanism that worked exactly as designed,

165
00:07:38,902 --> 00:07:41,235
one clean structural pass, and one real,

166
00:07:41,818 --> 00:07:44,235
project-redirecting flaw in what the flag actually measures.

167
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,375
The missing-number problem turned out to be duplicated in three separate files,

168
00:07:48,625 --> 00:07:49,425
not one.

169
00:07:49,583 --> 00:07:52,792
The same regex, copied three times, all with the same blind spot.

170
00:07:53,250 --> 00:07:56,333
All three got widened the same way, an unfixed duplicate left behind

171
00:07:56,333 --> 00:07:58,042
would have been worse than not fixing it at all.

172
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:01,000
The flag problem needed an actual decision, not a patch.

173
00:08:01,375 --> 00:08:04,667
The honest constraint: nothing in this system currently knows which real-world

174
00:08:04,708 --> 00:08:07,208
concept a number came from, only that a number exists.

175
00:08:07,958 --> 00:08:11,458
So a genuinely complete fix wasn't available without much bigger changes.

176
00:08:11,875 --> 00:08:15,042
The decision that shipped: a number cited by only one side no

177
00:08:15,042 --> 00:08:17,000
longer counts as a contradiction by itself.

178
00:08:17,417 --> 00:08:20,458
That fixes the case where one agent simply wasn't asked about something.

179
00:08:20,792 --> 00:08:24,000
It does not fix two agents citing real, correct, unrelated numbers.

180
00:08:24,375 --> 00:08:26,833
That gap is named in the code, tested as a known limit,

181
00:08:27,042 --> 00:08:29,500
and left open on purpose, not quietly dropped.

182
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:32,453
The fix wasn't just tested against hand-built examples.

183
00:08:32,870 --> 00:08:36,578
It was checked against the same twelve real companies, recalculated, not re-run.

184
00:08:37,078 --> 00:08:39,453
Under the old rule: eleven of twelve flagged. Under the new rule:

185
00:08:39,578 --> 00:08:43,120
seven of twelve. Four companies, where one agent simply hadn't quantified anything,

186
00:08:43,287 --> 00:08:44,328
stopped being false alarms.

187
00:08:44,828 --> 00:08:47,745
The other seven, where both agents cited real numbers about different things,

188
00:08:47,745 --> 00:08:51,287
are still flagged. That's the honest, measured size of the fix. Not

189
00:08:51,287 --> 00:08:52,087
everything.

190
00:08:52,495 --> 00:08:54,412
Something specific and countable.

191
00:08:55,100 --> 00:08:57,017
Two separate questions, two separate answers.

192
00:08:57,475 --> 00:09:01,100
Does the machinery work? Real filings, real independent reasoning, a full audit

193
00:09:01,100 --> 00:09:04,058
trail, a guardrail that held on every one of twenty-four real calls.

194
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:06,100
Yes. Provably, not just theoretically.

195
00:09:06,850 --> 00:09:10,392
Does the flag reliably mean these two agents disagree? Not yet. Most

196
00:09:10,392 --> 00:09:13,600
of what it flags right now is agents discussing different things, not

197
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:15,100
agents contradicting each other.

198
00:09:15,683 --> 00:09:19,100
And the one real fabrication this system produced was caught by a

199
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:20,308
person reading the output,

200
00:09:20,725 --> 00:09:21,975
not by the system itself.

201
00:09:22,830 --> 00:09:26,830
A system built specifically to distrust fluent, confident answers just produced one,

202
00:09:27,122 --> 00:09:29,830
and then measured exactly how wrong it was, instead of hiding it.

203
00:09:30,163 --> 00:09:31,288
That's not a finished detector.

204
00:09:31,705 --> 00:09:34,288
It's a detector that's now told on itself, twice, in two days,

205
00:09:34,288 --> 00:09:37,122
once about a number, once about what its own flag actually measures.

206
00:09:37,580 --> 00:09:40,330
The smallest true claim, not the biggest: this proved the plumbing.

207
00:09:40,705 --> 00:09:41,997
It did not yet prove the judgment.

208
00:09:42,690 --> 00:09:45,065
The number that wasn't there was caught by a person, not the

209
00:09:45,065 --> 00:09:45,865
system.

210
00:09:45,857 --> 00:09:46,657
Signing off, Divij Pawar.
